+## 6. Dated note, 2026-10-02 (lane R9) — the suffix collided, and §1 did not
+
+This is an addition, not a revision. Sections 1–5 above are as their author
+left them. What follows records a decision R8 correctly declined to make
+inside its own pass, and three measurements that correct its report.
+
+### 6.1 The brief's premise was half wrong: three broken readers, not three
+
+R8 measured three committed readers broken by the 45 markers it added. The
+collision surface is **six** sites. Resolved against each site's real root
+directory rather than by pattern match alone:
+
+| site | pattern | markers matched |
+|---|---|---|
+| `reviews/probes/inv_a_baseline_gaps.py:71` | `use-*.json` | 6 |
+| `experiments/ad01/s09_e1_gates_probe.py:90` | `use-*.json` | 6 |
+| `reviews/probes/ad01_bdf12d5_review.py:74` | `use-*.json` | 6 |
+| `reviews/probes/closure_84d2094.py:78` | `use-*.json` | 6 |
+| `scripts/inv01_study.py:585` | `export-*.json` | 6 |
+| `reviews/probes/ad01_bdf12d5_review.py:88` | `*.json` | 6 |
+
+The sixth is the one that decides the argument. `ad01_bdf12d5_review.py` also
+walks `committed.glob("*.json")`, and **no marker name that ends in `.json`
+can escape a `*.json` glob**. R8's proposed repair —
+`'use-w[0-9]-[IR].json'` — is narrower than `use-*.json` and would survive it,
+but the `*.json` walk at line 88 would still swallow six markers. A repair
+that fixes five of six sites and reports the lane green is worse than the
+breakage, because it looks fixed.
+
+### 6.2 The suffix cannot be renamed out of the way, and the reason is structural
+
+I tested eight candidate namings against all 52 committed glob patterns. Any
+name retaining a `.json` ending is matched by `*.json`; every non-`.json`
+ending is matched by `rglob("*")` at `scripts/checkpoint.py:132` and
+`tests/test_ad01_env.py:58`. There is no filename that is invisible to both
+classes.
+
+`rglob("*")` is a false positive on measurement: `_artifact_manifest` and
+`_committed_tasks` receive runtime-supplied directories and
+`worlds.FROZEN_DIR`, neither of which is an evidence tree. So a rename *is*
+reachable — `.supersession` (no `.json`) escapes every real reader. I did not
+take it, for the reason in §6.3.
+
+### 6.3 Why the readers changed instead: the rename destroys the mechanism
+
+§1's rule is that reachability comes from a sibling **named to sort beside the
+file it describes**. Rename the suffix and three things break at once:
+
+1. The marker stops being a `.json` file, so a marker is no longer openable by
+   the reader the mechanism exists for.
+2. §1's own worked example, `e3-postfix-ladder.json` →
+   `e3-postfix-ladder.supersession.json`, is the naming contract. Changing the
+   suffix changes the contract every future marker is written against, to fix
+   a bug that has one correct location.
+3. The 4 committed markers, the 30 tests, and `reports/workstreams/w3-e3-recompute.md`
+   all pin the current name.
+
+The collision has one cause and it is not the suffix. It is that readers
+enumerate a directory by **artifact family** (`use-*.json`, `export-*.json`)
+while the marker is named by **artifact stem**, and a name derived from a stem
+necessarily matches its own family's glob. The suffix is not the defect; the
+absence of a reader-side rule is. So the fix is one function —
+`artifact_paths()` in `scripts/evidence_supersession.py`, `glob(pattern)` minus
+markers — used by all six sites.
+
+**§1 is strengthened, not weakened.** Before, "no new file matches any glob
+pattern the tree is scanned with" was true only because no marker had been
+placed beside an artifact any reader globs. It was a census finding, not a
+rule. It is now a rule that holds by construction, and it is tested.
+
+### 6.4 The `EVIDENCE_ROOT` defect: 18 markers, invisible, and not clean either
+
+The brief says the 18 `evidence-ad01/` markers "all validate under a direct
+`check_marker` call". **They do not.** Measured: all 18 report `stale marker`,
+for the same CRLF reason as the other 31. `EVIDENCE_ROOT` became
+`EVIDENCE_ROOTS = ("reports/evidence", "evidence-ad01")`; `iter_markers` now
+returns **49** of 49. `check_evidence_tree` was reporting a clean tree it had
+never looked at.
+
+### 6.5 The digest: blob-digest is right, `.gitattributes` is still owed
+
+R8 pins `artifact_sha256` to the **git blob digest** and reports all 49 stale.
+Both halves are correct and they are not in conflict:
+
+- Pinned digest == blob digest: **49 of 49**.
+- Pinned digest == on-disk digest: **0 of 49**.
+
+**The correction.** The brief says the *prior* lane's marker pinned the CRLF
+on-disk digest, so `.gitattributes` would not help it. It pinned the blob
+digest too — all four committed markers, including the one whose §2 excerpt
+quotes `2d59e0c9…`, which is `sha256(blob)`, not `sha256(CRLF)`. So
+`.gitattributes` helps all 49 equally, and the claim that one marker is beyond
+repair is wrong.
+
+Measured, over 485 blobs under `reports/evidence/` + `evidence-ad01/`: **0
+contain CRLF**, and 482 of 485 on-disk files are exactly the blob with
+`\n`→`\r\n`. The whole discrepancy is `core.autocrlf=true` with no attribute.
+Adding `reports/evidence/** text eol=lf` and `evidence-ad01/** text eol=lf` to
+`.gitattributes` — the same scoped rule the file already carries for the
+content-hashed trees — makes disk bytes equal blob bytes on re-materialisation.
+I did not commit that change: it re-writes every working-tree copy of both
+evidence trees, which is a bigger decision than this note, and this lane is
+additive-only.
+
+**The position.** The blob digest is correct and stays. A digest that changes
+with the host's line-ending policy is a digest of the checkout, not of the
+source, and the existing `.gitattributes` preamble says exactly that. What is
+owed is the two-line attribute, not a re-stamp.
